01:14:11 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You're absolutely right—you weren't making it up. In fact, that's one of the most common and stubborn myths in biology education, and it's been reinforced by centuries of textbook diagrams, anatomical models, and even well-meaning teachers. So let’s directly address why the diagram showed blue veins and red arteries, and why your teacher taught it that way, while still explaining what's really happening. --- ### The textbook color coding is a convention, not a depiction of reality For centuries, medical and biology illustrators have color-coded blood vessels in diagrams: - **Red** = arteries (carry blood away from the heart, usually oxygen-rich in the systemic circulation). - **Blue** = veins (carry blood back to the heart, usually oxygen-poor in the systemic circulation). This is a **schematic convention**, not an attempt to show the actual color of the blood inside. It's like how wiring diagrams show electrical wires in different colors even though the real wires might all be copper. The contrast makes learning the pathways easier. The problem is that most textbooks never explicitly say "this is a color code; actual blood is never blue," so students (and sometimes teachers) naturally take it literally. Your teacher then passed on what the diagram seemed to show: veins = blue, arteries = red. She may have learned it that way herself and never had cause to question it, because it *feels* true when you look at your own wrist. --- ### Why the myth feels so real: blood looks blue through the skin, and "blue" is culturally linked to "lack of oxygen" You've already experienced that veins look blue. Combine that with the fact that we *do* say someone "turned blue" when they lack oxygen (cyanosis). It's an easy, intuitive leap: low oxygen → blue. But cyanosis is actually a bluish *tint to the skin*, not the blood itself turning blue. It's caused by a high proportion of deoxygenated hemoglobin in the capillaries making the overlying skin look dusky or bluish—again, an optical effect in the tissues, not transparent blue fluid flowing inside. --- ### A direct proof that you can see in real life If you've ever had blood drawn, or watched a medical procedure on TV, the blood that comes from a vein flows into a vacuum tube or syringe. As you noted earlier, the blood is always a dark maroon/purplish-red, never blue. It doesn't rush out blue and then turn red on contact with air—it comes out dark red and then *brightens* if exposed to oxygen. If the blood itself were blue, that would be captured on video constantly, but it never is. Likewise, during surgery, surgeons see veins directly. They never report blue fluid—just dark red blood. --- ### Why you can trust the corrected version This issue is well-studied and universally accepted in modern biology and medicine. The misconception persists in education because: - Textbook diagrams prioritize clarity over realism (and changing a centuries-old convention is slow). - The optical illusion is so compelling that it overrides abstract knowledge. - Many high school curricula simplify things, and not every teacher revisits the nuances they learned decades ago. You're not at fault for believing it—you were taught it by a trusted authority using visual aids that seemed to prove it. The real surprise is that the whole system let that oversimplification harden into "fact" for so many people. So in summary: Your memory of the blue/red diagram is perfectly accurate, and it's exactly why the myth is so widespread. But the actual fluid in all your veins is never blue—it's just a very dark red that, when viewed through skin, plays a trick on your eyes.
